GAME LOOP

two networking modes
- lockstep mode for normal card gameplay
    - game mode designates zero or one card grid per player
    - players can select zero or more cards within their grid (with locally-applied constraints)
    - since each player can have different views of each grid, this is evaluated from the perspective of each player separately
    - players send a reliable ordered list of card indices that they selected, and once all players have submitted their response to all other players, the game progresses until the next input prompt
    - players send unreliable state notifications to all other players while selecting inputs
    - players can revoke their submitted inputs
        - only if the other players are not "ready" yet and must wait for all other players to reliably acknowledge the revocation before it becomes real
        - if the player reaches a state where it appears that all other players are ready during this process, they reliably send the same input that they originally revoked
        - this can also be done if the player cancels their revocation. the only valid input during a revocation state that has not been fully confirmed is the input being revoked, which cancels the revocation
        - if a player receives a revocation request after they believe everyone to be ready, they send a reliable non-acknowledgement packet
- rollback mode for realtime gameplay
    - rollback packets received during lockstep mode are ignored, so pseudo packet loss is expected at the start of a rollback segment
    - players keep a log of inputs (ABXY, d-pad, select, start) per frame
    - inputs are run-length encoded and sent to all other players
    - the history is repeatedly unreliably sent to all other players, along with a counter for the other player's last received input
    - the game can pause and wait for players to catch up if the last received input counter for a player is too far behind the current player's (adjusted by ping)
    - two states are kept; the latest known frame that all players agree on and the current frame for the current player
    - when an input in the past is received, the early state is either updated (if all players have submitted that frame's input) or copied to the current state and updated back to the current player's current frame
    - if a player's input is unknown, it is assumed to be the same as their previous input, with the first input on the frame when rollback begins being "all buttons released"
    - differences between the previous and next "current" state must be accounted for. animations update to the point where they would be had the input been known all along. sounds can be cancelled or started already partially through their runtime if needed.

network state transitions
- initial setup happens using reliable packets
- lockstep mode can start rollback mode at any time
- rollback mode can end itself only when all players have confirmed the frame where rollback ends
- when rollback mode ends, it rolls back to the frame where it would have ended in perfect networking conditions
- at any time, a player can crash, desync, or quit, resulting in the match ending with a partial recording and an error report

DATA FORMAT

container
- magic: starts with the bytes 61 a4 1c 87 b7 7e 0e 30 (first 8 bytes of sha256("Spy Cards Online"))
- all fields are uvarints unless otherwise specified
- field: format revision (currently always 0; for future expansion)
- field: container type (enum)
    - GAME_MODE_SUMMARY = 0
    - GAME_MODE = 1
    - RECORDING = 2
    - ERROR_REPORT = 3
- field: game version (3 uvarints; for the first version of godot spy cards online this will be 0, 4, 0)
- field: timestamp (unix timestamp in milliseconds; upload or modification time for game mode + summary, match start time for recording + error report)
- field: number of embedded files
- repeated (count = number of embedded files):
    - field (bytesvar): file id
    - field (bytesvar): content
    - file id must be unique in this list
    - file id must be one of the local types (local PNG, local OPUS, local GLTF)
    - when a mode is uploaded, these are converted to uploaded types and replaced within the mode
    - cannot upload recordings with any embedded files (but they can be saved locally)
- remaining fields are specific to container type

