network protocol (overview)
- matchmaking server server-side parts already implemented (see server/signaling-server-2.go in the parent repo)
- connection handshake is godot's webrtc implementation (not specified here)
- game uses reliable and ordered unreliable RPC messages for communication
- players are connected in a mesh configuration (every player is talking directly to every other player, so for a match with n players, there are (n)(n-1)/2 total connections, (n-1) per player)
- technically allows up to 255 bug players (as opposed to bot), but good luck getting that many
- all information is known to all computers to simplify implementation and calculations
- initial random seed is generated on player 1's computer
- random seed for each round is generated by concatenating random bytes from each player
- only inputs, randomness, and checksums are sent; all other data and computations are done locally for each player

network protocol (init)
- RELIABLE PEER: all players send service worker cache hash upon connect and compare to their own; if mismatch, the match is aborted and players are told to refresh
- RELIABLE PEER: p1 sends initial random seed, match init timestamp, game mode data, and any embedded files to all other players upon connect
- RELIABLE BROADCAST: each player broadcasts their display name and chosen character once they have selected
- RELIABLE BROADCAST: each player broadcasts their initial deck once they have selected
- Note: if a player connects after a broadcast has occurred, that information is sent to them upon connect.
- Once a player has sent both broadcasts and received both broadcasts from every other player, the match begins, initially looping in lockstep mode.

network protocol (lockstep):
- The match is simulated until a CHOICE command is reached.
- The state of the match is saved and every operation until the CHOICE command resolves is reverted and re-applied every time any input data changes.
- RELIABLE BROADCAST: when the CHOICE command is reached, each player generates 4 random bytes and computes a checksum of the match state. This data is sent to all other players. If the checksum differs, the match is aborted. The random seed that will be used after the CHOICE command is resolved is the initial random seed concatenated with the random bytes from each player, in player number order.
- Note: CHOICE commands select zero or one card grid for each player; each player then selects zero or more cards in order. The game mode is able to specify a function that computes whether a given choice is valid and update the UI locally. Unlike previous versions of Spy Cards Online, the choices are ordered.
- UNRELIABLE BROADCAST: whenever the player's choice is changed, they send their current list of selections to all other players. this is ignored if the player has confirmed their choices reliably. the game mode can use this information to adjust the UI to indicate which choices the opposing players are in the process of making.
- RELIABLE BROADCAST: when the player's choices are confirmed, the list of selections is sent to all other players.
- RELIABLE BROADCAST: if a player wants to go back to choosing after they have confirmed, they can request that other players un-confirm their choices.
- RELIABLE PEER: when a revert request is received, as long as the CHOICE command has not resolved yet, send back a message indicating acknowledgement of the request. if the CHOICE command has resolved already, send back a message indicating refusal of the message.
- RELIABLE BROADCAST: if refusal is received or all other players have confirmed their choices before all the acknowledgements have been received, the player sends the same choices that they initially tried to revert. otherwise, if the revert succeeds, the player goes back to choosing normally.

network protocol (rollback):
- RELIABLE BROADCAST: When a REALTIME command is reached, players synchronize random bytes and checksums the same way as in lockstep mode
- Once all players have synchronized random bytes and checksums, rollback mode begins.
- Inputs (up, down, left, right, a, b, x, y, select, start) are recorded at 60Hz.
- Two copies of the game state are kept: the "ground truth" state, which is simulated up until the inputs that have been received for all players, and the "future" state, which is simulated as if all players had kept holding the buttons they are currently holding until the time of the local player's latest input.
- REALTIME mode can only end in the "groud truth" state; the "future" state pauses if it reaches the end of REALTIME mode.
- UNRELIABLE PEER: Players send to each peer: their current starting frame number, the last frame number they have received from the peer, a run length encoded list of inputs since their starting frame, and the last two checksums of the ground truth state, computed at an interval of two times the maximum frame count difference.
- A player's starting frame number for a given peer is the last acknowledged frame number they have received from that peer.
- If the maximum frame count difference is reached, the game pauses and tries to wait for clocks to sync back up [TODO: check how GGPO does this].
- RELIABLE BROADCAST: When the REALTIME command terminates, players synchronize again and the match logic continues in lockstep mode.

